    "title": "Time Management",
    "topic": "time management at university",
    "audioUrl": "/audios/SGD_12_Time_Management.mp3",
    "transcript": "Narration: Three students are discussing time management at university.\n\nSpeaker1: There are a lot of challenges for us at university. It’s not just doing well in the courses; we have to be able to balance the academic work with a personal life and also find time to join some extracurricular activities. I mean I don’t want my years at university to be only studying and writing papers.\n\nSpeaker2: I know. There’s just so much to do. I have to get to the lectures, do all the readings and assignments, complete several projects, and get ready for exams. I hardly have time to breathe.\n\nSpeaker3: You know, it’s true. My biggest challenge since I started university is managing my time. I can’t seem to get myself organized and I always feel stressed and exhausted. And the worst thing is that I just seem to get organized and the term ends and I have to get used to a whole new schedule.\n\nSpeaker1: I agree. That is really difficult. Each course has its own schedule, deadlines, and requirements. It’s so difficult to create a consistent routine so I can allocate my time effectively. And everything seems to take longer than I thought.\n\nSpeaker2: The worst part is that I often procrastinate and put off tasks until the last minute. I think to myself, oh I have lots of time for that, I have a whole week before it’s due. Then all of a sudden, the due date is in two days and I’m rushed and don’t do a very good job.\n\nSpeaker3: That’s happened to me so many times. The deadlines are weeks or months away and I have trouble with long-term planning. It’s hard to plan so far ahead but you have to or you end up rushed. The problem is that you have to learn to multi-task and I was never very good at that. I like to finish one project and start on another. But that’s impossible when all the projects and essays are due at the same time at the end of the term. You really have to be able to multi-task at university, you have to be able to jump from one topic to another and one type of assignment to another.\n\nSpeaker1: All these tight deadlines, diverse course requirements and multi-tasking pressures make university time management far more difficult than I expected. It’s really hard to stay stable and efficient when so many different tasks pile up at the same time.\n\nSpeaker2: If I could manage my time better, I probably wouldn’t be so stressed and exhausted. I’ve never had to discipline myself like this before. In high school, the teachers told you what to do and when to do it. If you just did what they said, you were fine. But in university, it’s up to you to prioritize what tasks are the most important and should be done first. It seems I spend too much time on less important tasks because I like them or they are easier, then I don’t have time for the more important ones.\n\nSpeaker3: I guess not managing your time effectively means that you end up working more than you really need to and you feel so tired and sort of burnt out. I think the university offers a seminar on time management. Maybe we should all go.",
    "keyPoints": "*解析\n\nKey points\n\nTopic : time management at university.\n\n主题：大学里的时间管理与学业生活平衡。\n\nSpeaker 1\n- 观点：大学不仅是上课拿分，更需要平衡学业、个人生活与课外社交活动。\n\nSpeaker 2\n- 观点：繁重的课业阅读、大作业和考试准备让人喘不过气，常常缺乏充分休息时间。\n\nSpeaker 3\n- 观点：难以有条不紊地规划日程，刚适应一个学期节奏又迎来全新课表，心理压力巨大。\n*",
    "modelAnswer": "The discussion involves three people talking about time management at university.\nThe speaker one first mentioned that balancing academic study with personal life is very challenging.\nAnd then, the speaker has explained that students want to find time for extracurricular activities.\nThe second speaker is mainly about feeling overwhelmed with lectures, readings, and course projects.\nHe also mentioned that students hardly have time to rest before preparing for exams.\nAs for the third speaker, she highlighted that struggling to organize schedules causes stress and exhaustion.\nWhat's more, the speaker suggested that constantly adapting to new semester timetables adds pressure.\nIn conclusion, they all agreed that developing effective time management habits is critical for college life.\nOverall, they focused on the main topic of time management at university.",
