    "title": "Complaint in Library",
    "topic": "library noise problem",
    "audioUrl": "/audios/SGD_20_Complaint_in_Library.mp3",
    "transcript": "Narration: Two students and one librarian are having a discussion in the library, focusing on the noise problem, rule enforcement and suggestions for improving the learning environment.\n\nSpeaker1: The library is supposed to be a quiet, peaceful place for people to focus on their studies, finish assignments, or prepare for exams. But recently, many students in the library have been talking loudly, laughing, and even chatting freely with their friends, making it feel more like a busy social club than a place for academic work.\n\nSpeaker2: I am well aware of this situation, and I completely understand your frustration. However, their noisy behavior is quite difficult for us to manage effectively. As you can see, our library is quite large, with multiple floors, reading areas, and public spaces, and we simply do not have enough staff to monitor every corner and every student’s behavior at all times. Perhaps you could consider moving to the first floor or the computer area, where the atmosphere is a bit more relaxed.\n\nSpeaker3: Unfortunately, the computer area isn’t any better. Many students there watch sports games, entertainment videos, or even live streams on their computers, and they often play the audio very loudly without using headphones. On top of that, their conversations are also quite noisy. All this noise makes it just as hard for us to concentrate on our work as the main reading area, if not harder.\n\nSpeaker1: I see what you mean. Maybe a clearer division of areas would help. Perhaps it would be more effective to clearly state the rules. For example, small talking should be allowed in the computer area. Meanwhile, in the main reading area and silent study rooms, people should try not to talk at all, not even in a low voice. This way, students will know exactly where it is acceptable to talk.\n\nSpeaker2: Actually, talking is already allowed in our computer area. As for the silent area, if you ever feel distracted by noise there, you can call the security desk directly for help.\n\nSpeaker1: I think the library should strengthen its promotion of these rules.\n\nSpeaker2: That’s a very good suggestion. You’d better write down your complaints and suggestions in detail. The university attaches great importance to students’ opinions.\n\nSpeaker3: That’s good to know, but why do some people bring drinks into the library? Is that permitted? I’ve seen students spill their drinks on the tables and chairs several times, leaving stains that are hard to clean.\n\nSpeaker2: Bringing drinks into the library is actually not allowed. Drinks can easily be spilled, making the library environment messy and untidy, and they can also cause damage to books, tables, and other public facilities.",
    "keyPoints": "*解析\n\nKey points\n\nTopic : library noise problem.\n\n主题：大学图书馆噪音干扰与环境治理投诉。\n\nSpeaker 1\n- 观点：备考期间图书馆内私下耳语与接听电话严重干扰注意力，急需安静自习环境。\n\nSpeaker 2\n- 观点：小组讨论区与独立静音区未完全物理隔断，管理人员应当严格巡视并执行安静纪律。\n\nSpeaker 3\n- 观点：建议推广隔音研讨舱预约机制，明确分区标识，兼顾交流讨论与深度专注两种需求。\n*",
    "modelAnswer": "The discussion involves three people talking about the library noise problem.\nThe speaker one first mentioned that loud whispering and phone calls disturb exam revision.\nAnd then, the speaker has explained that students need a quiet environment to concentrate.\nThe second speaker is mainly about separating group discussion areas from silent zones.\nShe also mentioned that library staff should strictly enforce silence regulations.\nAs for the third speaker, he highlighted that booking soundproof study pods helps collaborative groups.\nWhat's more, the speaker suggested that clear zoning signs maintain a comfortable reading space.\nIn conclusion, they all agreed that improving library management ensures productive study environments.\nOverall, they focused on the main topic of library noise problem.",
