    "title": "Graffiti Art",
    "topic": "graffiti and street art",
    "audioUrl": "/audios/SGD_32_Graffiti_Art.mp3",
    "transcript": "Narration: three students are discussing graffiti and street art.\n\nSpeaker1: When I was walking towards the train station yesterday, I passed an old factory wall covered with graffiti. I just stood there looking at it, and honestly, it was amazing. The paintings were so detailed and colorful that it almost felt like an outdoor art gallery. When I walked towards the train station, there was an old factory wall with graffiti on it.\n\nSpeaker2: Yes, but isn’t it illegal? Even if it isn’t, I think it probably should be. Personally, I’m against it because it’s basically vandalism. A lot of people do it during the night when there are no police around, and usually they even have someone watching out for them.\n\nSpeaker3: I agree to some extent. There are other places where they can paint and express themselves, and nowadays there are also online platforms. Artists can post their work on the Internet instead of painting freely on public walls without permission.\n\nSpeaker1: But I wasn’t talking about random writing or tagging. I mean actual paintings of people on the street, wearing sunglasses and things like that. Some of them are incredibly creative. The artwork was so imaginative that it brightened up the entire neighbourhood. I stood there for quite a while just looking at them and thinking. It made the whole area feel more alive and interesting.\n\nSpeaker2: I understand what you mean, but surely there should be designated places for that kind of art. Artists could also sell their work online or display it legally in galleries instead of painting on factory walls without permission. There is no need for them to paint privately on factory walls or public buildings.\n\nSpeaker3: Personally, I don’t like the idea of doing absolutely everything online. Seeing artwork in real public spaces feels different. It creates an atmosphere that you can’t really experience through a screen. It offers a real, immersive experience that digital art simply cannot replicate.\n\nSpeaker1: Exactly. I think artists should have some freedom to express themselves in public spaces, especially when the artwork actually improves the environment instead of damaging it. It is a special form of urban culture worth preserving.\n\nSpeaker2: Maybe. But I still think there should be rules and designated areas. I actually know an artist who paints legally in public spaces, and his work is still very popular without causing problems for anyone.\n\nSpeaker3: I think that is a perfect middle ground. Balancing artistic freedom and public rules is really important. Legal street art zones allow creators to express their ideas freely, enrich urban scenery and promote local culture, while also preventing random vandalism and property damage. It satisfies both artistic development and public management needs.",
    "keyPoints": "*解析\n\nKey points\n\nTopic : graffiti and street art.\n\n主题：关于街头涂鸦艺术与城市公共秩序的辩论。\n\nSpeaker 1\n- 观点：废弃厂房墙面的精美涂鸦极具色彩冲击力，宛如户外露天美术馆，富有艺术感染力。\n\nSpeaker 2\n- 观点：未经许可涂鸦本质属于破坏公物（Vandalism），常在深夜躲避巡逻作案，应当予以取缔。\n\nSpeaker 3\n- 观点：表达自我应有界限，可在指定的合法艺术区或上传网络平台展示，而非随意涂画公私墙体。\n*",
    "modelAnswer": "The discussion involves three people talking about graffiti and street art.\nThe speaker one first mentioned that factory wall graffiti feels like an outdoor art gallery.\nAnd then, the speaker has explained that he appreciates creative artistic expression.\nThe second speaker is mainly about unauthorized street graffiti being illegal property vandalism.\nShe also mentioned that people paint at night without police supervision.\nAs for the third speaker, he highlighted that artists should paint in legally designated spaces.\nWhat's more, the speaker suggested that online platforms allow sharing artwork without defacing public walls.\nIn conclusion, they all agreed that artistic freedom must respect public property laws.\nOverall, they focused on the main topic of graffiti and street art.",
